<b><font size=4>WILDLIFE ABOUNDS</font></b>
<p>
<b>Steve Bauer</b><br>
<i>Pocket Water, Inc. (PWI)</i>
<p>

The consultants have observed a noticeable increase in ducks and wading birds on the Wildlife Management Area (WMA).  Sandpipers, plovers, killdeer, and ducks are more plentiful than in the past.  The project has increased the area of placid water and lengthened the period that the water remains in the floodplain during the spring.  Large numbers of invertebrates, which provide food for wading birds, can be observed in the shallow water areas.  Although we have no empirical measurements, it is logical that this enhanced habitat area has contributed to the increase in waterfowl observed on the WMA

	</td>
	<td width="50%" valign="top">
	
<b><font size=4>REPAIR THE CHUTE ... NOT</font></b>
<p>
<b>Denny Dawes</b><br>
<i>Wildlife Habitat Institute (WHI)</i>
<p>
Despite the TAC decision to repair the downstream end of the 1997 construction area (affectionately called the "chute" or "boat launch"), Mother Nature again had her way.  With the deluge of rains, water in the river channel topped the downstream reinforced bank of Giant Bend.  Water then flowed over the newly constructed floodplain and down the "chute", deeming it impractical to do any work.  We will show a videotape at the September TAC meeting.  The mini track excavator is no longer available.  We will consider the feasibility of a 2nd attempt for repairs later in the field season.

	</td>
